In the example, we see that we have the tests for Add, Remove, Insert, Set, Get, Contains, IndexOf and LastIndexOf methods. For each method we are assuming a precondition that the ArrayList is initialized, observing the values before and after the operation, and making various assertions about the expected behavior and the actual results.

With the PexFramework you can write automated UI tests, service tests, or even database tests using the unit tests framework. You can write your tests as an ordinary method, but mark that method with one of the Pex method attributes, like, PexMethod, Action or TestMethod. 

With an input specification for your code, Pex will automatically explore all possible paths through your code. And not just that, but all the possible data it can generate for your input. It generates a large test baseline and provides you with an explicit list of what your tests will actually cover.

Here are some of the things you can do with Pex:

 1. Verify that your code behaves correctly by verifying the contracts of methods.
 2. Generate a lot of values automatically and Pex will find the interesting ones for you.
 3. The system will only test the interesting paths through your code.
 4. Verify that your code behaves correctly by generating random data and running your tests.
 5. Get your tests covered by Pex by marking your method with the PexMethod attribute.
 6. Pex will even generate random values for your variables, which you can then use in your method to test how your variable performs.
 7. Pex will explore your code in depth and generate a large number of test cases in combination with the many possible values that can be generated. This will save you many hours of manual testing. 
